      "bodyTr": "Justice Minister nominee Lee Yong-chul evaded the question once again during his parliamentary confirmation hearing yesterday regarding whether he would exercise his authority to dismiss the prosecution of President Lee Jae-myung's criminal case. When asked about his intentions to order a dismissal of charges, the nominee repeatedly answered that he had no intention of exercising prosecutorial authority in a manner the public would find difficult to accept. He went so far as to respond \"Yes, that's correct\" when pressed on whether he could commit to not ordering a dismissal, stating that \"the prosecution will decide on the dismissal on its own, and I will not direct it. Can't I say that?\"\n\nWhen asked whether he received requests from the Presidential Office to dismiss charges during his appointment process, he dodged the question by saying it would be inappropriate to comment. Given his remarks, suspicions can only grow that he ultimately intends to order the dismissal. Prior to the hearing, the nominee consistently maintained that clarifying the truth about allegations of manipulated prosecution takes priority, while everything else will follow the law and principles. This time too, he said he would not exercise improper authority, but he did not clarify what that standard would be.\n\nUnlike his predecessor Justice Minister Jung Sung-ho, who was negative about ordering a dismissal of charges, this nominee's refusal to draw a clear line against the possibility of dismissal cannot be considered appropriate for a justice minister who must uphold the rule of law. The ruling party and the nominee are arguing that if the truth about the manipulated prosecution allegations is revealed, dismissing the charges would be a natural consequence. However, their premise is flawed from the start. The very structure in which the president appoints a special prosecutor to handle a case related to himself and uses that special prosecutor to investigate allegations of manipulated prosecution undermines public trust in the special prosecutor.\n\nIf the ruling party is so confident about the manipulated prosecution claims, they could simply ask for a judgment based on evidence and legal principles in court. President Lee recently posted on social media expressing grievances about how the recovery of 550 billion won in public benefits from the Daesan-dong development project could possibly constitute breach of trust. This inevitably invites criticism that he issued implicit instructions to both the special prosecutor and the justice minister nominee. The nominee is a close associate who worked at the same law firm as President Lee and served as the head of an organization under Seongnam City. Controversy arose from the time of his appointment about whether he was selected with the intention of dismissing charges in President Lee's case. If, as these concerns suggest, the criminal case of a sitting president is closed in the prosecution based on political judgment, it would undermine the very foundation of South Korea's criminal justice system. The nominee must clearly state that he will not exercise his authority to dismiss charges in the president's case and must keep that commitment.",
