        "byline": "EDITORIAL",
        "body": "Jensen Huang’s return lifts Seoul stocks, yet policy flaws and chip reliance remain A market buoyed by one man’s return has a peculiar definition of confidence. South Korea’s Kospi offered a demonstration on Tuesday, rebounding to just above 5,000 after losing nearly half its value from its June peak, one day after Nvidia reinstated co-founder Jensen Huang as chief executive. The recovery followed a punishing retreat. After a June closing high of 9,114.55 and an intraday peak of 9,385.59, the Kospi lost nearly half its value amid doubts over returns on vast artificial intelligence investments. Huang’s return sent Nvidia shares up 31.5 percent on Monday, their biggest daily gain in more than two decades, lifting global chip stocks. Huang’s comeback, however, cannot settle the questions surrounding the AI boom. He faces congressional scrutiny over his alleged relationship with a woman suspected of being a Chinese agent. US lawmakers are considering tighter restrictions on advanced chip exports to China, while data center delays could disrupt the spending plans underpinning global chip demand. For Korea, the implications reach beyond Nvidia’s boardroom. Samsung Electronics and SK hynix have benefited from AI memory demand, driving exports and the Kospi’s ascent. Their success has left the country’s growth outlook dependent on investment decisions by a handful of US technology companies. Doubts over the economics of data center investment have quickly become concerns about Korean exports and corporate earnings. Chip gains lifted the headline index even as many listed companies lagged. Policymakers treated record highs as evidence of a broader revival, encouraging decisions that reinforced the advance ahead of the June local elections. Authorities allowed leveraged and inverse exchange-traded funds tied to individual chipmakers, magnifying daily bets. The National Pension Service postponed domestic stock rebalancing and raised its domestic-equity target from 14.9 to 20.8 percent. Such steps crowded investors into an overheated trade. When prices turned, the pension fund had less room to adjust, while households that borrowed heavily to buy shares faced steep losses. A public pension should pursue long-term, risk-adjusted returns, rather than serve as a prop for the government’s market ambitions. Political leaders cannot claim credit for rising share prices and dismiss a collapse as temporary. By Friday, declining stocks had outnumbered advancing ones by more than two to one for the first time since the Lee Jae Myung administration took office, exposing the weakness beneath the index. The official response has been hesitant. After an emergency meeting on Sunday, financial authorities announced preparations to activate the 10.8 trillion won ($8 billion) stabilization fund, a three-month easing of daily limits on corporate share-buyback orders and tougher checks on illegal short selling. Established in 2020 during the COVID-19 pandemic, it has never been deployed. Its pledged capital would be called from contributors only after activation is approved. Finance Minister Lee Hyoung-il said Monday that authorities would decide after reviewing market conditions, without specifying a trigger or timetable. Taiwan, also dependent on semiconductors, moved earlier this month to authorize deployment of a stabilization fund worth roughly 20 trillion won and tighten short-selling curbs. Seoul need not copy every measure, but the comparison shows the value of a credible backstop prepared before panic sets the pace. Even if promptly deployed, the stabilization fund would only buy time. Authorities should disclose clear activation criteria and keep pension decisions tied to investment objectives. The government also needs to reassess fiscal assumptions built around an exceptional semiconductor cycle, and address regulatory and labor-market barriers to productive investment. Tuesday’s rebound offered Seoul’s stock market a welcome reprieve, but did little to restore investor confidence. Nor did it establish that AI spending can withstand current doubts or that Korea can absorb a slowdown in chip demand. Lasting confidence rests on broad-based corporate strength at home, not a boardroom reversal abroad. Seoul shares down over 5% late Wed. morning on tech rout South Korean shares extended their losses late Wednesday morning, reversing the previous session's rally, as investors tracked overnight losses in technology stocks on Wall Street. Expulsions, 10-year bans eyed to combat AI exam cheating No scalp photos, no insurance: Hair loss patients face fivefold rise in drug costs Kim Ju-ae’s luxury ski gear draws US scrutiny Samsung, LG expand mini-LED TV lineups as Chinese rivals push upmarket [Reporter's Notebook] Where was education minister amid Suneung cheating scare? Reporter's Notebook From paychecks to parcels, a guide to sending things home from Korea Survive & Thrive True Crime Take a stroll through the darker side of Korea’s past Herald Interview A series of in-depth interviews. The Ultrarich Lifestyle of the ultrarich Stars Up Close A spotlight on Korean artists who rose to prominence in unexpected ways, and moments behind their breakthroughs [Editorial] The credibility test [Lee Byung-jong] Korea's lofty AI ambitions On this day in Korea - November 21, 1997 : Korea seeks IMF bailout [Editorial] After the Suneung scramble [Lim Woong] The end of test scores as a measure of merit Expulsions, 10-year bans eyed to combat AI exam cheating Actor Ko Kyung-pyo reports to police for questioning on drunk-driving crash BTS back in military uniform? Here's what happened In South Korea, 'Swedish massage' can mean more than a massage Jensen Huang’s return eases HBM uncertainty for Samsung, SK hynix",
